Subject: [PATCH] innodb: use fsync() instead of fdatasync() under Wasmer

Works around a Wasmer bug, not a WASIX limitation. Observed with Wasmer
7.3.0 (WASIX sysroot v2026-07-30.1, wasixcc 0.4.5), the newest release on
2026-08-29: fdatasync() on a file that another thread is concurrently
writing eventually never returns, and once it has stalled the process can
no longer exit either. fsync() under the same concurrency is fine.

A small standalone WASIX program reproduces it without MySQL: one thread
pwrite()s 8 KiB blocks to a file while a second thread loops fdatasync() on
the same descriptor, with a watchdog thread that reports when both counters
stop moving. The fsync() variant completes 4000 writes every time; every
variant with fdatasync() anywhere stalls within seconds, whether or not a
second descriptor to the file is involved.

InnoDB's redo log writer and flusher are exactly that pair of threads. With
innodb_use_fdatasync=ON the flusher stalls inside fdatasync() while holding
the flusher mutex, the writer blocks on that mutex when it marks the first
redo file full (log_files_produce_file), and --initialize hangs silently
with every thread idle: 8.4 buffers the error log until after InnoDB
initialization, so nothing is printed. 8.4 defaults innodb_use_fdatasync=ON,
so this patch is required there; earlier versions default it OFF and hit the
hang only when the flag is set.

srv_use_fdatasync is consulted in exactly one place, os_file_fsync_posix(),
so compile the pre-existing fsync() fallback (the branch upstream uses where
configure finds no fdatasync()) on wasm builds. The system variable still
exists and can be set; it has no effect.

After upgrading Wasmer, re-run the reproduction described above (or
build with this patch omitted and run the MySQL smoke test); if fdatasync()
returns, this patch can be dropped.

diff --git a/storage/innobase/os/os0file.cc b/storage/innobase/os/os0file.cc
index 7a23b9f4..ce0e0222 100644
--- a/storage/innobase/os/os0file.cc
+++ b/storage/innobase/os/os0file.cc
@@ -2829,11 +2829,16 @@ static int os_file_fsync_posix(os_file_t file) {
     meb_mutex.unlock();
 #endif /* UNIV_HOTBACKUP */
 
-#if defined(HAVE_FDATASYNC) && defined(HAVE_DECL_FDATASYNC)
+#if defined(HAVE_FDATASYNC) && defined(HAVE_DECL_FDATASYNC) && \
+    !defined(__wasm__)
     const auto ret = srv_use_fdatasync ? fdatasync(file) : fsync(file);
-#else  /* !(HAVE_FDATASYNC && HAVE_DECL_FDATASYNC) */
+#else  /* !(HAVE_FDATASYNC && HAVE_DECL_FDATASYNC) || __wasm__ */
+    /* Under Wasmer 7.3.0, fdatasync() racing with a write to the same file
+    from another thread eventually never returns, and the redo log flusher
+    and writer are exactly that pair. fsync() does not stall, so
+    innodb_use_fdatasync is ignored on WASIX. */
     const auto ret = fsync(file);
-#endif /* !(HAVE_FDATASYNC && HAVE_DECL_FDATASYNC) */
+#endif /* !(HAVE_FDATASYNC && HAVE_DECL_FDATASYNC) || __wasm__ */
 
     if (ret == 0) {
       return (ret);
